.TH LOCATION 3 hosted
.SH NAME
location \- the host's location service as an NMEA receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #g/nmea
.B #g/status
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Under
.IR emu (1),
.B #g
gives the location service of the host it runs on (a phone's) as a
GPS receiver would: a stream of NMEA 0183 sentences, which
.IR gpsfs (4)
takes as it takes a serial receiver:
.IP
.EX
gpsfs source '#g/nmea'
.EE
.PP
.B nmea
is read-only.
Each open gets the sentences from then on, and each read waits for
more.
For each position the service gives there are three sentences: an RMC
(time, date, position, speed in knots, course), a GST (the accuracy,
a radius, as equal latitude and longitude errors whose root sum of
squares is the radius, and the altitude's error) and a GGA (position
and altitude above mean sea level).
A value the service does not give is left empty.
The service says nothing of satellites: GGA's count and dilution are
empty, and there is no GSV.
.PP
The first open of
.B nmea
starts the service and the last close stops it, so it runs only while
something reads.
The open fails if the host has none, giving the reason.
.PP
.B status
is the service's state:
.B unavailable
(none on this host),
.B stopped
(nothing reads),
.B waiting
(started, no position yet, or a permission the user has yet to
answer),
.BR ok ,
.B denied
(the permission refused),
.B off
(location services turned off).
.SH HOSTS
On Android it is the LocationManager's fused provider (Android 12 and
later; GPS before), asking for the location permission when the
application starts; the altitude is given only where Android gives
one above mean sea level (Android 14 and later).
On iOS it is CoreLocation, asking for permission (while in use) the
first time
.B nmea
is opened.
.PP
On other hosts the device is there and says
.BR unavailable ,
unless the environment variable
.B INFERNODE_LOCATION_FAKE
was set when the emulator started:
.IP
.EX
INFERNODE_LOCATION_FAKE='lat lon [alt [accuracy]]'
.EE
.PP
in degrees and metres, which gives a fix a second from there, moving
north a metre a second; it is for tests and for trying a map on a
desk.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devlocation.c
.br
.B /emu/port/locationbridge-stub.c
.br
.B /emu/Android/locationbridge.c
.br
.B /android-app/app/src/main/java/io/infernode/InfernodeLocationBridge.kt
.br
.B /emu/iOS/locationbridge.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR eia (3),
.IR nmea (2),
.IR gpsfs (4)
.SH BUGS
There is none on Windows.
.br
macOS's CoreLocation is not used: it grants location only to an
application bundle, and
.I emu
run from a terminal is not one.
